# Async and Lifecycle Rules

- Report orthogonal outcomes independently. A result can be several things at once; surface each independent fact on its own instead of nesting one outcome inside another's branch.
- Honor public contracts on both sides. Normalize multiple internal representations of one outcome before returning through the public API, document the normalized contract where its type is defined, and exercise every source form through the real consumer.
- Async state is not synchronous per-operation state. Define the interval an owner controls explicitly, and handle the “nothing to wait for” branch so waits cannot hang.
- Represent one asynchronous operation with one lifecycle controller or transaction. Separate readiness, cancellation, disposal, reservation, or sentinel state requires an independent owner or settlement point; otherwise fold it while preserving rollback, callback containment, and quiescence.
- Dispose must reach quiescence, not just request it. Make cleanup asynchronous, await child completion, and close listener or notification registries before stopping children so late completions stay silent.
- Contain callback exceptions in the dispatcher. One bad subscriber must not reject core lifecycle work or starve later listeners.
